---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import portrait from "../../assets/work/articles/cayce-reese.jpg";

const tags = ["Business", "Oconee State Bank", "Banking", "Storytelling"];
---

<Base
  title="Q&A: Cayce Reese on Community Banking, Storytelling | Grace Joy Park"
  description="Grace Joy Park's beat Q&A with Cayce Reese, marketing and business development coordinator at Oconee State Bank, on community storytelling and financial honesty."
>
  <p class="back"><a href="/work">&larr; Back to Writing</a></p>

  <article class="post">
    <h1>Q&amp;A: Cayce Reese on Intersection of Community Banking, Storytelling</h1>
    <p class="meta muted">Reporting I, Grady College &middot; Feb. 14, 2026 &middot; By Grace Park</p>

    <ul class="tags">
      {tags.map((t) => <li>{t}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <figure class="hero">
      <Image
        src={portrait}
        alt="Cayce Reese, marketing and business development coordinator at Oconee State Bank"
        widths={[600, 900, 1200, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 900px"
        format="webp"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Cayce Reese, marketing and business development coordinator for Oconee State Bank.
        Reese transitioned from an executive assistant role to help lead the bank&rsquo;s
        community storytelling and branding. (Photo/David McFarland)
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        Cayce Reese serves as the marketing and business development coordinator for
        Oconee State Bank. Sitting at the intersection of local finance and community
        storytelling, Reese offered a unique perspective on how community banks stay
        competitive in a digital age. She discussed the balance between profit and
        customer service, the importance of financial honesty for young professionals
        and the critical role integrity plays in the future of business.
      </p>

      <p class="qa-q">
        <strong>Q: What did you learn about the bank&rsquo;s mission while working at the
        executive level? How does that make you a better storyteller?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        It honestly transforms the way you see things when you sit in those strategic
        meetings. You hear the executives explain the vision and not just the fun story
        elements. As a storyteller, I do not just focus on the feelings. It has to drive
        results. I think it is a beautiful combination to have the organizational skills
        and the strategic understanding.
      </p>

      <p class="qa-q">
        <strong>Q: When you meet small business owners in Athens or UGA students, what
        would your advice be when managing money?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a really good book called &ldquo;Unreasonable Hospitality.&rdquo; It
        talks about how you have to prioritize what matters. Manage your operational
        costs well, but then find that 5 to 10 percent where you can have a little bit
        of pizzazz. It is preplanned spontaneity that allows for the wow moments that
        happen.
      </p>

      <p class="qa-q">
        <strong>Q: What skills matter most to you looking into the future of business?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Integrity. When you sit in spaces where there is a high level of trust, people
        are trusting you with major decisions that impact every single day of their
        lives. It is not a small thing. Even with AI, if you cannot navigate AI with
        integrity then we are in trouble.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="full-link">
      <a href="/work/cayce-reese-qa.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        Read the full Q&amp;A (PDF) &rarr;
      </a>
    </p>

    <section class="why-section">
      <h2>Why I Wrote The Story</h2>
      <p>
        Community banking sounds dry until you talk to someone like Cayce Reese, who
        frames money as a story about integrity and &ldquo;preplanned spontaneity.&rdquo;
        The Q&amp;A format let her advice on financial honesty stand on its own without
        me narrating over it, which is a skill I wanted more reps on. It also deepened
        my business beat by opening Oconee State Bank as a recurring source for
        small-business stories.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .back { margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem; }

  .post h1 {
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }

  .meta {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .tags {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }

  .tags li {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .hero {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  .hero :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .hero figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.5;
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .prose p {
    max-width: 900px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .prose .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
  }

  .prose .qa-q {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  .full-link {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2.5rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .why-section {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    max-width: 900px;
  }

  .why-section h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .why-section p {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
